<!-- From -->
Ubiq had the following to say on the UWOO@egroups.com mailing list concerning the realism vs. immersion debate:
<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
For the record, I'm not criticizing you or your ideas, just 
explaining how we look at these problems.  I prefer to think
of it as a challenge - you are quick to point out the problems - 
so I challenge you to offer a better answer.  You don't need
to be a game designer to know what's fun in a game, but you 
do need the ability to see the problem from the point of view
of the thief, the inn owner, and the inn patron. =)
<p>
That being said, let's talk about realism.  Often, when making
things 'realistic', you need to do things in a way that would
seem counter-intuitive.  For example, you point out that it
is unrealistic that you can't pick up a book in a store.  I
would argue, contrarily, that a world where every store, library
and inn in the world had their furniture stolen daily is far
less realistic.  A world where no one decorates their shop or
inn because it's not worth risking losing the decorations and 
going through the hassle is also not realistic.  If you look 
at it that way, adding the ability to lock down items certainly 
adds realism. =)
<p>
A better goal is immersion, in my opinion.  For example, when
Trespasser came out, it attempted to have a 'realistic' interface
with a hand that you could use in different ways to manipulate
different things.  This hand was difficult to understand, 
hard to control, not to mention pretty ugly to look at.
<p>
Now compare this to Unreal.  In Unreal, you run over items to 
pick them up.  Very unrealistic, technically, right?  However,
what this simple interface does is it removes this part of the
gameplay from your thought, so you can instead focus on the 
parts of the game that ARE realistic (physics, projectiles,
level design - the strengths of a good first person shooter).
Immersiveness is all about playing off of your strengths to 
make the best possible gameplay, while doing your best to 
take the things that are your weaknesses out of the way.
The fact that you never THOUGHT about how you pick up items
let that brainpower be better spent on things that are more
immersive and more fun. =)
<p>
Of course, your mileage may vary. 
<!-- /Indent --></font></td></tr></table><!-- End /Indent -->
<!-- End Story -->
<!-- Thanks -->
Thanks!
<!-- End Thanks -->
</font>


